<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Model Architecture</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Overall Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IQuest-Q1 builds on the Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> with sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and hybrid sliding-window attention (see <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 𝜇P: Transfer Across Width and Depth. ‣ 2.1 Overall Architecture ‣ 2 Model Architecture ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>). The attention stack interleaves Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and Sliding Window Attention (SWA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Within each attention module, a query-aware sink token <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> provides an adaptive destination for excess attention mass. Query–key normalization (QKNorm) stabilizes attention by controlling query and key scales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and QKNorm Clip is additionally applied to prevent excessively large affine parameters. Partial RoPE applies rotary position embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> to only a subset of the dimensions in each attention head. On the feed-forward side, the first Transformer layer replaces sparse MoE routing with a dense MLP to mitigate expert load imbalance in the earliest layer of the model.
To improve scaling effectiveness, IQuest-Q1 adopts Stable SplitNorm to increase effective depth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. For efficient and stable scaling, we combine the Muon optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P parameterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>, supporting efficient optimization and learning-rate transfer across model sizes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stable SplitNorm.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional Pre-Norm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> may underutilize the depth of the model. As the unnormalized residual stream accumulates across layers, its variance can grow with depth, reducing the relative contribution of later residual updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>. Motivated by these observations, IQuest-Q1 adopts a hybrid normalization design introduced in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>. We refer to this design as <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SplitNorm</em>, which splits the normalization strategy across Transformer sublayers: the residual stream entering the attention sublayer is normalized, whereas the residual stream entering the feed-forward network (FFN) is kept unnormalized. This design combines the stability benefits of Pre-Norm with the stronger depth utilization associated with Post-Norm, mitigating the underutilization of deeper layers in Pre-Norm architectures. Given an input <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the SplitNorm block used here is formulated using RMSNorm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\mathrm{RMSNorm}(x),\qquad h=z+\mathrm{Attention}(z),\qquad y=h+\mathrm{FFN}\bigl(\mathrm{RMSNorm}(h)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Attention</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>FFN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\mathrm{RMSNorm}(x),\qquad h=z+\mathrm{Attention}(z),\qquad y=h+\mathrm{FFN}\bigl(\mathrm{RMSNorm}(h)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input hidden state, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the RMSNorm-normalized input, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is the intermediate representation after the attention block with a residual connection, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the output after the FFN block with another residual connection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This hybrid design combines normalized attention states with an identity residual path around the FFN. However, directly scaling this formulation to large models can lead to unstable optimization. To address this issue, IQuest-Q1 further introduces two modifications. First, an RMSNorm is applied to the attention output to prevent activation explosion, such that the attention update in <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Stable SplitNorm. ‣ 2.1 Overall Architecture ‣ 2 Model Architecture ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 1</span></a> becomes <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=z+\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\mathrm{Attention}(z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Attention</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=z+\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\mathrm{Attention}(z))</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, a SandwichNorm structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, implemented with RMSNorm, is used for the first layer to stabilize the model input at the beginning of the residual stream.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P: Transfer Across Width and Depth.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IQuest-Q1 adopts <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P parameterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite> for width-wise learning-rate transfer. Depth-wise transfer depends on additional parameterization choices, as studied in Depth-<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> and CompleteP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Under our adopted parameterization, empirical validation demonstrates hyper-parameter transfer across models spanning a <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> range in width and a <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> range in depth. Instead of tuning the learning rate directly at the target scale, we perform the search on a small proxy model with a hidden width of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="896" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>896</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">896</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> layers. The learning rate selected on this proxy is then transferred zero-shot to the target model under the adopted parameterization, without additional learning-rate tuning at the target scale. We provide detailed <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P scaling factors in <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 𝜇P scaling factors. ‣ Appendix A Pre-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30949v1/M1-ARCH-V3.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:362/321;" width="362" height="321"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overall architecture of IQuest-Q1.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Recursive Multi-token Prediction.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve backbone learning through additional future-token supervision, we incorporate two multi-token prediction (MTP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> layers from the start of pre-training. After completing the training pipeline, we adapt the first layer into a recursive drafter for speculative decoding and discard the second layer in a dedicated training stage. As in EAGLE-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, we extend the speculation horizon with a fixed parameter budget by reusing the same MTP layer across seven drafting steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>. In each drafting step, the output hidden state and generated token are fed back into the same MTP layer to produce subsequent predictions. We also replace its attention with SWA using a smaller window, which bounds the per-step attention cost as the context grows. Details can be found in <a href="#A1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Recursive Multi-token Prediction. ‣ Appendix A Pre-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 11</span></a> in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Scaling Efficiency and Stability</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scaling efficiency.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate scaling efficiency, we conduct a controlled comparison between IQuest-Q1 and a representative Qwen3-MoE-style design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> following the Qwen3-30B-A3B configuration, using identical training data.
As shown in <a href="#S2.F4.sf1" title="Figure 4(a) ‣ Figure 4 ‣ Scaling stability. ‣ 2.2 Scaling Efficiency and Stability ‣ 2 Model Architecture ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>, IQuest-Q1 consistently achieves lower training cross-entropy loss at a given training FLOPs budget. Overall, IQuest-Q1 exhibits a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.93\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.93</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.93\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher scaling efficiency than the baseline, requiring <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/1.93\approx 52\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.93</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>52</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/1.93\approx 52\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the training compute to reach the same training loss. These results demonstrate the cumulative benefit of the proposed model design under controlled training conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scaling stability.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the Critical Batch Size (CBS) perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, we propose a complementary framework for characterizing the scaling limit imposed by training stability. CBS identifies the largest batch size that can be used without substantially degrading optimization efficiency; analogously, our framework aims to identify the largest model size that can be trained under a given architecture and optimization recipe without sacrificing either stability or optimization efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a model of size <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider two learning-rate boundaries: the <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimal learning rate</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives the most efficient optimization under a fixed training budget, and the <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safe learning rate</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the largest learning rate that remains within the stable training regime. We provide the definition and evaluation criteria for stable training in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" title="Safe learning rate. ‣ Appendix A Pre-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. The available stability headroom can be quantified as:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{N}=\log\frac{\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}(N)}{\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}(N)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{N}=\log\frac{\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}(N)}{\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}(N)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A positive <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the efficiency-optimal operating point remains within the stable regime, while <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{N}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{N}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that optimization has become stability-constrained. We therefore define the <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Critical Model Size (CMS)</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{CMS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{CMS}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the model scale at which the optimal and safe learning rates coincide:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}(N_{\mathrm{CMS}})=\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}(N_{\mathrm{CMS}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}(N_{\mathrm{CMS}})=\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}(N_{\mathrm{CMS}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This provides a compact measure of how far a given architecture and training recipe can scale while simultaneously maintaining training stability and optimization efficiency, serving as a proxy metric for the model’s scaling stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.F4.sf2" title="Figure 4(b) ‣ Figure 4 ‣ Scaling stability. ‣ 2.2 Scaling Efficiency and Stability ‣ 2 Model Architecture ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>shows the scaling trends of both learning rates observed in our experiments. For IQuest-Q1, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains substantially above <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> across the measured scales. Extrapolating the fitted trends yields <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{act}}\approx 572" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi><mi>act</mi></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><mn>572</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{act}}\approx 572</annotation></semantics></math>B and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{total}}\approx 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi><mi>total</mi></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{total}}\approx 12</annotation></semantics></math>T, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{act}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi><mi>act</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{act}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>CMS</mi><mi>total</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{CMS}}^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the CMS measured by activated and total parameters, respectively. These results indicate substantial stability headroom for scaling IQuest-Q1 beyond the model sizes studied.</p>
</div>
