<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Infrastructure</h3>

<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Packing.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sequence packing reduces padding overhead in language-model training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. To improve effective token throughput at a fixed sequence length and global batch size, we apply best-fit packing to the globally shuffled data stream using a bounded look-ahead window. The window contains up to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2BS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2BS</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the global batch size and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> the sequence length. Samples left unpacked in the current window are carried over and prioritized in the next iteration, preventing them from being indefinitely delayed by subsequent packing decisions.
Long-context training amplifies compute imbalance arising from document-length variation. Packed sequences are therefore reordered across data-parallel replicas to balance computation at micro-batch granularity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sink Attention and Context Parallelism.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Context parallelism uses ring-based K/V exchange for full attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, while sliding-window attention exchanges only the required halo and overlaps these exchanges with computation in both the forward and backward passes. During halo exchange, each rank computes attention over its local K/V, producing outputs for interior queries and partial outputs with associated Log-Sum-Exp (LSE) statistics for boundary queries. Once the halo arrives, the rank computes the complementary contributions for boundary queries and combines the local and halo partial outputs through LSE-based renormalization. This allows boundary queries to make progress before remote K/V becomes available while preserving sliding-window attention semantics. We illustrate the halo exchange schedule and partial output merging
in <a href="#A1.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ SWA linear context parallelism with partial halo attention. ‣ Appendix A Pre-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 10</span></a> in the appendix. Both full and sliding-window attention execution paths fuse learnable sink contributions into attention computation to reduce memory traffic.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Correctness Validation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce silent errors introduced by model and training changes during RSI, the agent mines semantic invariants from the model’s training setup. These invariants are converted into checks that run during distributed training to detect deviations from single-machine semantics and violations of predefined correctness constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Pre-Training</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Pre-Training Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a high-quality, multilingual pre-training corpus spanning diverse domains, drawing from web pages, public GitHub code, books, academic papers, news, and domain-specific materials. For the code, we preserve diversity across programming languages, modalities, and task granularities, dimensions emphasized by prior work on multilingual code generation, multi-granularity evaluation, and repository-level executable completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>. Our unified data processing pipeline includes cleaning, quality filtering, exact deduplication, and fuzzy deduplication to reduce noise and redundancy while preserving diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">General pre-training proceeds in two stages with a progressively adjusted data mixture.
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1</span> establishes foundational language capabilities and general knowledge through diverse corpora, maximizing unique token counts across multiple domains. <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2</span> gradually increases the proportion of code and STEM data to strengthen programming, mathematical, and scientific problem-solving capabilities. Specifically, we design a four-phase pipeline increasing code data percentage from 40% to 70%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Mid-training then combines high-quality natural corpora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, QA pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>, and agentic trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> to prepare the model for downstream reasoning and agentic tasks. Across pre-training and mid-training, the model is trained on a total of 29T tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Hyper-Parameters</h3>

