<td id="S3.T1.5.1.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 10.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.13.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MTP Sliding Window Size</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 10.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.T1.5.1.13.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (Inference)</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As summarized in <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Model Hyper-Parameters. ‣ 3.2 Hyper-Parameters ‣ 3 Pre-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>, IQuest-Q1 has <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>88</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88</annotation></semantics></math> Transformer layers and a hidden dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{,}072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,072</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{,}072</annotation></semantics></math>. Attention uses <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48</annotation></semantics></math> query heads and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> key–value heads, each with a dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>. The first two and last two layers use full attention (FA) to capture global information and improve training stability, while the intermediate layers repeat three SWA layers followed by one FA layer, yielding a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{:}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{:}1</annotation></semantics></math> local-to-global ratio within each repeating block. The SWA uses a window size of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4{,}096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4,096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4{,}096</annotation></semantics></math>. Partial RoPE is applied to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions of each attention head, with RoPE bases of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> for SWA and FA, respectively. Query-Aware Sink Attention is enabled in all attention layers.
The first Transformer layer uses a dense FFN with an intermediate dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12{,}288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12,288</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12{,}288</annotation></semantics></math>, while the remaining layers use sparse MoE FFNs. Each MoE layer contains <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> routed experts, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> experts activated per token and an intermediate dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}536" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,536</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}536</annotation></semantics></math> for each expert. Both dense and expert FFNs adopt SwiGLU. The vocabulary size is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>160,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>, with untied input embeddings and output weights.
During pre-training, IQuest-Q1 adopts two Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) layers to improve the performance of the backbone model. Each prediction order uses an embedding dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> attention heads, and the MTP loss is weighted by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Hyper-Parameters.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ Muon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> for all parameters except token embeddings, the language modeling head, normalization parameters, and MoE routers, which are optimized with Adam. For Adam, we set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-15}</annotation></semantics></math>. For Muon, we set the momentum to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. For an <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math> weight matrix, we scale the orthogonalized update by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2\sqrt{\max(m,n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2\sqrt{\max(m,n)}</annotation></semantics></math>, combining spectral scaling with an additional factor of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> to align the update RMS with that of Adam-style updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. We apply weight decay with a coefficient of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> to all parameters except layer normalization parameters, which are exempt from weight decay. We additionally apply QKNorm Clip, capping the affine parameters of QKNorm at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>. We adopt a warmup-stable-decay learning rate schedule. The learning rate is warmed up to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and held constant for the remainder of Stage 1, then decays to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> by the end of Stage 2 and to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> by the end of mid-training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Mid-Training and Long-Context Extension</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Training Curriculum</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mid-training bridges broad pre-training and specialized agent post-training through a curriculum that combines a diverse data mixture, selective token-level supervision, and progressive context extension.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Organization.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mid-training mixture comprises three principal categories: high-quality natural corpora, QA pairs, and agentic trajectories. We organize training data using a chat template adapted for mid-training, retaining a selected subset of special tokens, including those associated with reasoning and tool calls.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Token-Level Loss Masking.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We specify loss masks directly for individual tokens, independently of the role-specific special tokens used to determine supervision in a typical post-training setup. This decouples data formatting from loss computation and allows supervision to be tailored to selected spans within each training example, with finer control than masking entire conversational roles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Progressive Context Extension.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the maximum training context lengths through 32K, 128K, and 512K tokens, using variable-length sequence packing throughout. The global-attention RoPE base increases from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500,000</annotation></semantics></math> at 32K to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,000,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,000,000</annotation></semantics></math> in the subsequent phases; the hybrid attention pattern, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4,096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4,096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4,096</annotation></semantics></math> sliding window, and SWA RoPE base of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> remain fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optimization across Phases.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mid-training learning rate follows a single cosine decay across all three context-length phases, with no additional warmup. At phase transitions, we transfer model weights and reinitialize the optimizer state while preserving the learning-rate schedule’s progress.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Agentic Data Construction Guided by Trajectories</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic mid-training requires large and diverse trajectory corpora, but suitable trajectories are scarce and concentrated in a limited set of tasks and environments. To expand coverage while preserving realistic interaction patterns, we use selected trajectories as behavioral anchors and pre-training documents to broaden technical and task coverage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using these sources, we construct executable, containerized instances from compatible code repositories, following prior work on tasks derived from repositories and trajectory generation at scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>. This process yields more than one million instances. We roll out an agent on each instance and retain valid and complete trajectories for mid-training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate pre-training and mid-training using two complementary types of evaluations. First, we compute <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bits-per-byte (BPB) on held-out agent trajectories</span>. Lower BPB indicates that the model assigns higher likelihood to behaviors observed in competent agent trajectories, providing a generation-free measure of how well the model captures the trajectory distribution. This metric can provide a fine-grained training signal that complements downstream task-level evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further decompose long-horizon agent behavior into <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">atomic abilities</span> and evaluate how reliably the model performs each ability. This fine-grained evaluation helps identify specific capability gaps and informs data selection and curriculum design. Together, these two evaluation paradigms provide complementary signals for tracking and improving agentic capabilities during pre-training and mid-training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Post-Training</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Large-Scale and Diverse Task Synthesis</h3>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30949v1/instance_distribution.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/281;" width="476" height="281"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of coding-agent task instances and their environments, verifiers, and construction methods.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We organize the task synthesis framework into three settings: general, reasoning, and coding.
Each setting uses diverse data sources and a domain-specific pipeline to generate tasks at scale.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General Agent.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collect APIs and MCP servers from RapidAPI<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://rapidapi.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://rapidapi.com</a></span></span></span> and Smithery<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://smithery.ai" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://smithery.ai</a></span></span></span>, public skills from the web, and workspace files for task synthesis. Guided by the Standard Occupational Classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, we cover a broad range of occupations and synthesize high-coverage environments and tasks, which are iteratively refined through a solve-then-refine loop.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reasoning Agent.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We synthesize tasks through a document-grounded pipeline that jointly controls concept coverage, material quality, and task difficulty. The pipeline first expands a broad concept inventory into fine-grained scientific topics, then retrieves and curates documents for each topic, filters the documents before synthesis, and finally converts the retained materials into tasks with reference answers. By covering high-value knowledge through the document collection, the pipeline promotes corresponding coverage in the synthesized prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coding Agent.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct executable environments from GitHub repositories and industrial data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>, <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> using multiple versions of coding-agent harnesses such as Claude Code and Codex, with our model driving environment construction and skills supporting setup. Task queries draw on PRs and issues, repository knowledge graphs and documentation, and reusable patterns extracted from cleaned, de-identified usage logs. Skills capture task structure and common failure modes to guide instance synthesis. Verifiers combine agent-written tests, adapted repository tests, and checks derived from documented behavior. For offline instances, external dependencies are replaced by local fixtures or mocks. An agent-judge loop checks executability, leakage, and consistency among instructions, reference solutions, environments, and verifiers, repairing defective instances and rejecting weak assertions. Reference solutions must pass while no-op solutions must fail. Rubric-based trajectory review further filters cheating and low-quality execution beyond binary success. Repeated agent runs estimate difficulty, and overly easy instances are enriched with additional skills or constraints before revalidation; valid instances exposing model failures are retained.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An overview of coding-agent task instances is shown in <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Large-Scale and Diverse Task Synthesis ‣ 4 Post-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a>. We construct instances through two complementary modes: building new environments from raw sources and updating verified environments through re-tasking, rewriting, fault injection, evolution, hardening and repair, and selective re-rolling. The resulting library covers software engineering, interactive and multimodal tasks, cybersecurity, and skill-grounded professional work, with domain-specific runtimes and verifiers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.30949v1/IQuest-Q1-Post-Training.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/140;" width="476" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">IQuest-Q1 Post-Training Pipeline.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Environment Integrity and Reward Hacking Mitigation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic training environments are vulnerable to reward hacking, where models exploit leaked solutions or weaknesses in task verifiers instead of solving the intended tasks. Such behavior can contaminate SFT data and introduce misleading reward signals during RL. To prevent this, we apply three layers of protection: environment preparation, execution-time checks, and trajectory auditing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Environment Preparation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before collecting trajectories, we prepare task environments to prevent solution leakage. Depending on the task, we clean Git history, remove files that could reveal existing solutions, or create fresh environments containing only the required materials. We also isolate task environments to prevent unintended access to hidden evaluation data or resources from other tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution-Time Checks.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During rollouts, we restrict access to external resources and sensitive task files. Depending on the environment, we enforce network restrictions or intercept tool calls before execution. We develop blocking rules from observed hacking behaviors to prevent agents from retrieving existing solutions, upstream code, or hidden evaluation materials. These checks preserve access to legitimate dependencies and task resources. Blocked operations return explicit feedback, allowing agents to continue without the prohibited access.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Trajectory Auditing.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further audit collected trajectories using rule-based checks and rubric-guided LLM judgments. The audits examine executed commands, tool outputs, and submitted solutions to identify behaviors such as retrieving leaked fixes, accessing hidden tests, or modifying evaluation scripts. Confirmed hacking trajectories are excluded from training, while suspicious cases can be flagged for further review. We also use detected cases to refine our environment restrictions and blocking rules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-Specific Safeguards.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adapt these safeguards to the needs of each task. For SWE-like tasks, we keep only the Git history up to the base commit and use hooks in Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode to block attempts to retrieve existing solutions. For Terminal-Bench, we remove Git history when it is not needed and check commands before execution when it must be preserved. ProgramBench takes a different approach, building fresh containers with only the compiled oracle and task documentation, without exposing the original repository.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Coding Agent. ‣ 4.1 Large-Scale and Diverse Task Synthesis ‣ 4 Post-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>, we initialize supervised fine-tuning (SFT) from the mid-trained checkpoint to prepare the policy for reinforcement learning. We optimize the autoregressive cross-entropy loss on assistant responses, including tool-call tokens, while masking user instructions and tool observations from the loss. SFT proceeds in two stages (240B+ tokens in <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1</span> and 50B+ tokens in <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2</span>), with initial learning rates of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and cosine learning-rate decay within each stage. Multiple samples are packed into each sequence, with attention masking to prevent cross-sample attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Environment-Centered Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We outline an RL design that targets verifiable task completion while accounting for user constraints and execution cost. It draws on configurable-harness training in Kimi K3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and domain-specialized Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) in DeepSeek-V4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-harness, Multi-version Episodes.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve the model’s performance across different agent harnesses and versions, we train a shared policy on trajectories collected from diverse harness configurations, including Claude Code, Codex, and their different versions.
Each episode specifies a task domain, harness and its version, environment, and evaluator.
During training, the policy interacts with different harnesses, observing public instructions, tools, and execution feedback while reference solutions and hidden checks remain isolated.
GRPO compares repeated rollouts of the same task under a fixed harness and evaluator version. Sampling balances domain–harness combinations and retains regression cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rewards and Failure Attribution.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Independent verifiers determine terminal correctness. For long trajectories, auxiliary segment-level rewards are restricted to measurable progress, while penalties address unnecessary computation, constraint violations, and reward hacking. Model-generated diagnoses require evidence from execution records before contributing to these signals. Failures are attributed before optimization: policy-caused errors supply learning signals, environment or harness faults unrelated to policy behavior are masked, and evaluator faults block sample consumption. Token-level updates use recorded rollout probabilities through the existing Token-In, Token-Out (TITO) pipeline. The resulting specialized policies can subsequently be consolidated through MOPD.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Coding Agent. ‣ 4.1 Large-Scale and Diverse Task Synthesis ‣ 4 Post-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>, we initialize the student from SFT Stage 2 and distill knowledge from four
frozen expert models:
(1) an <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agentic user experience expert</em> for terminal
interaction, tool use, web development, and coding workflows;
(2) a <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-harness agentic expert</em> for operation across
Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode;
(3) a <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">long-horizon agentic expert</em> for extended coding
and cybersecurity tasks; and
(4) a <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">general agentic expert</em> for working-agent tasks,
web search, multi-turn conversation, and reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following MOPD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, we collect <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts from
<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}=p_{\theta_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}=p_{\theta_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and select a frozen teacher <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for
each task <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> according to its domain, <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=d(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=d(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given the interaction context <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the teacher evaluates
student-generated tokens to obtain a clipped distillation advantage:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{a}_{i,t}=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(\log\frac{q_{d_{i}}(y_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t})}{p_{k}(y_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t})},-c,c\right),\quad c&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{a}_{i,t}=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(\log\frac{q_{d_{i}}(y_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t})}{p_{k}(y_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t})},-c,c\right),\quad c&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Motivated by reverse-KL distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>,
we optimize the length-normalized surrogate:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{k}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\frac{m_{i}}{T_{i}}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{A}_{i}}\tilde{a}_{i,t}\log p_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{k}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\frac{m_{i}}{T_{i}}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{A}_{i}}\tilde{a}_{i,t}\log p_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains model-generated token positions,
including tool-call tokens, and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}=|\mathcal{A}_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}=|\mathcal{A}_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Instructions and tool observations provide context but are
excluded from the loss.
We set <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for repetitive rollouts and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
Rollouts, contexts, advantages, and masks are fixed during
differentiation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Model Merging</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Coding Agent. ‣ 4.1 Large-Scale and Diverse Task Synthesis ‣ 4 Post-Training ‣ IQuest-Q1: Advancing Foundation Capabilities for Agentic CLI Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>, starting from SFT Stage 1, we interleave training with residual model merging across stages and within expert branches, ultimately incorporating expert and MOPD checkpoints into IQuest-Q1. Each merge combines parameter deltas relative to a designated reference checkpoint using prescribed weights and global <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm calibration, then adds the resulting update back to the reference parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.7 </span>Post-Training Infrastructure</h3>

<section id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-harness and Multi-version Agent Runtime.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A single policy can be trained on trajectories from multiple harnesses and versions within one run.
Each training instance specifies its scaffold, task environment, and verifier. The runtime executes the corresponding configuration and verifies the task in an isolated sandbox while preserving tool orchestration, context management, and interaction flow. We map different API protocols to a unified policy interface to reduce the difficulty of downstream integrations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rollout-training Consistency.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement an end-to-end TITO path at the model-serving boundary. Specifically, we apply a session-level prefix tree to retain token histories shared across turns and branches.
A request then combines its environment observations with a recorded token prefix. The corresponding response is returned in the structured format expected by the scaffold. Finally, we retain trajectories paired with rewards and token-level masks from the tree at the end of the sequence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Asynchronous Distillation and Teacher Execution.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement MOPD in a fully asynchronous training system. We apply periodic synchronization of student parameters and explicit control of sample staleness, limiting the policy lag introduced by asynchronous execution. We execute teacher forward passes with the training engine on trajectories generated online by the student, producing token-level teacher log probabilities.
We use the same execution path for teacher evaluation and student log-probability computation to reduce discrepancies caused by the training engine.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This framework supports independently configured teachers through a common scheduling interface. Teachers reuse the same execution resources through weight switching. We store teacher weights in distributed storage and load them on demand, with only the shards required by the active teacher placed in GPU memory.
Weights of the next teacher are prefetched during the current forward pass with overlapping computation. This design supports additional teachers without a proportional increase in resident GPU replicas.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation</h2>

